//RS /// A scratch index no other in-flight oracle build can be using.
//RS ///
//RS /// #2020: `assert_cranelift_three_way` hard-coded index 0, so every stem's
//RS /// generated `.rs` landed on the same file name. That was invisible while the
//RS /// battery judged one stem at a time; two stems judged at once would overwrite
//RS /// each other's source. One counter, so every oracle build names its own
//RS /// scratch no matter which thread asked for it.
//RS fn next_oracle_index() -> usize {
//RS     static NEXT: std::sync::atomic::AtomicUsize = std::sync::atomic::AtomicUsize::new(0);
//RS     NEXT.fetch_add(1, std::sync::atomic::Ordering::Relaxed)
//RS }
//RS 
//RS /// Judge one curated stem list, each stem in its own child run of `test_name`.
//RS ///
//RS /// #2020: ten tests carried a private copy of this loop and every copy spawned
//RS /// its children strictly one at a time. That put ~160 optimized `rustc` builds
//RS /// in series inside `--test dev`, which is most of why the 900s suite guard
//RS /// aborted the binary before ~30 of its declared tests ever started. The
//RS /// children were already isolated — own process, own pid-keyed scratch, own
//RS /// thread-local JIT state — so the serialization bought nothing.
//RS ///
//RS /// The FFI bridge is the one build artifact the children share, and the product
//RS /// locks that cache per key across processes (#2075), so a bridge stem needs no
//RS /// suite-level serialization. Ten copies of one loop is also why their timeouts
//RS /// had already drifted apart with nothing naming the difference (AGENTS.md I8);
//RS /// the timeout is a parameter now.
