//RS 
//RS /// Judge the three-way universe in a bounded pool instead of one stem at a time.
//RS ///
//RS /// #2020: this battery pays an optimized `rustc` build per stem over ~350 stems,
//RS /// so on its own it outlasted the whole 900s suite budget and the binary never
//RS /// reached its own summary. Every leg is per-thread state — `jet_jit` keeps the
//RS /// trace flags, the program args and the resident module in thread-locals — and
//RS /// `collect_corpus_gate_records` already judges the same corpus with
//RS /// `test_worker_count(8)` workers doing the same three legs, so serial execution
//RS /// was never what kept the legs honest.
//RS ///
//RS /// Each worker enters `with_jit_test_scope` per stem: that is the product
//RS /// boundary that installs the sized compiler stack on THIS thread, which a
//RS /// default 2 MiB worker stack does not have.
//RS ///
//RS /// A failing stem is named and the rest are still judged, exactly as
//RS /// `run_interpreter_battery_parallel` does. The old walk stopped at the first
//RS /// divergence, so one failure hid every other one.
