//RS 
//RS /// Pin the CLIF Jet emits at a site that once produced verifier-invalid code
//RS /// (#1989, #1990, #1991).
//RS ///
//RS /// This failure class is INVISIBLE from stdout alone. Cranelift's verifier
//RS /// rejects the function, the tier silently deopts to the canonical
//RS /// interpreter, and the program still prints the right answer — which is
//RS /// exactly how the nine census members survived. Correct output is therefore
//RS /// necessary but nowhere near sufficient; four facts have to hold together:
//RS ///
//RS ///   1. the repaired site is actually inside the emitted program, so a green
//RS ///      run is evidence about *that* site (`jit_program_func_names`),
//RS ///   2. every emitted function passes the verifier —
//RS ///      `compile_cranelift_bundle` hands back the verifier's own text, so a
//RS ///      regression names its own instruction and both disagreeing types instead of
//RS ///      returning silently.
//RS ///   3. the tier plan binds the whole program to resident Cranelift, and
//RS ///   4. the run really executed native code: no deopt, no fallback.
//RS ///
//RS /// Invalid CLIF that Jet itself emitted is a compiler bug of I2's severity, so
//RS /// every one of these is a hard failure rather than a recorded gap.
// END-PORT-PENDING 1036 tests/dev_parts/support.rs:1807-3656

